WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.148
An auditor has a folder of twenty invoices, and four of them

00:04.248 --> 00:08.821
contain errors. She checks five of them at random. How many errors

00:08.921 --> 00:13.353
will she find? It looks like a binomial problem. It isn't quite.

00:15.450 --> 00:18.724
Two more distributions, each with a clear picture behind

00:18.824 --> 00:22.220
it: drawing without replacement, and counting rare events.

00:23.316 --> 00:26.383
The difference is that she doesn't put an invoice back

00:26.483 --> 00:29.433
after checking it. The first invoice she picks has a

00:29.533 --> 00:32.072
four in twenty chance of containing an error.

00:32.750 --> 00:35.919
But the second pick depends on the first. If the first

00:36.019 --> 00:39.612
had an error, three bad invoices remain among nineteen. If it

00:39.712 --> 00:43.850
was clean, four remain among nineteen. The trials are not independent,

00:43.950 --> 00:46.756
and p changes as she goes. So it isn't binomial.

00:46.850 --> 00:50.969
Instead we count directly: choose y of the four bad invoices,

00:51.069 --> 00:54.359
n minus y of the sixteen good ones, and divide by

00:54.459 --> 00:59.132
all the ways to choose five. That is the hypergeometric distribution.

01:00.216 --> 01:03.381
Here it is next to the binomial we might have used. With

01:03.481 --> 01:09.035
only twenty invoices the hypergeometric is noticeably tighter: sampling without replacement means

01:09.135 --> 01:11.950
each find makes the next one a little less likely.

01:12.050 --> 01:15.423
But make the folder bigger, keeping twenty percent of

01:15.523 --> 01:19.093
it faulty. With two hundred invoices, then two thousand,

01:19.193 --> 01:23.288
removing five barely changes anything, and the two distributions

01:23.388 --> 01:26.827
become the same. When the population is large compared

01:26.927 --> 01:30.235
to the sample, the binomial is a fine approximation.

01:31.333 --> 01:35.258
Now a different kind of count. An ATM sees on average three customers

01:35.358 --> 01:38.991
every ten minutes. How many will arrive in the next ten minutes?

01:39.083 --> 01:43.093
Chop the ten minutes into many tiny slices, so small that at

01:43.193 --> 01:47.136
most one customer can arrive in each. Then every slice is a

01:47.236 --> 01:51.041
trial, with a tiny chance of an arrival, and the count is

01:51.141 --> 01:53.507
binomial with a huge n and a tiny p.

01:53.600 --> 01:57.315
As the slices get finer, the binomial settles onto a

01:57.415 --> 02:02.085
fixed shape: the Poisson distribution, marked by the yellow dots.

02:02.185 --> 02:06.268
It depends only on lambda, the average count, here three.

02:06.368 --> 02:09.643
And its mean and its variance are both lambda.

02:10.733 --> 02:15.403
So: the hypergeometric counts successes when you draw without putting back,

02:15.503 --> 02:19.409
and it looks binomial when the population is large. The Poisson

02:19.509 --> 02:24.306
counts events built from many tiny independent chances, like arrivals, claims

02:24.406 --> 02:27.168
or outages, and its mean equals its variance.
